<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Queen Amina</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Amina of Zazzau</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Amina is remembered in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hausa">Hausa</a> tradition as a warrior queen of Zazzau, in present-day northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a>, usually placed in the 16th century. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kano-chronicle">Kano Chronicle</a> says that she conquered lands as far as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nupe-kingdom">Nupe</a> and Kwararafa and that tribute came to her from many towns. Earthen walls in the region are called Amina's walls, though historians regard much of her story as uncertain.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/queen-amina.jpg" alt="Queen Amina" width="320" height="526" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sidhant Bendre · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Queen_Amina_Statue_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
